## Initializing Variables

In C#, a variable must be assigned a value before it can be used.

For example, consider code that declares a string variable named `productDescription` and then tries to display the variable's value in a message box.

The problem is that no value has been assigned to the variable.

When the application is compiled, an error message may appear:

`Use of unassigned local variable 'productDescription'`

The C# compiler will not compile code that tries to use an unassigned variable.

## Declaring Multiple Variables with One Statement

You can declare multiple variables of the same data type with one declaration statement.

Example:

```csharp
string lastName, firstName, middleName;
```

You can break up a long statement so that it spreads across two or more lines.

Example:

```csharp
string lastName = "Khalaf",
       firstName = "Mohamed",
       middleName = "Abdullahi";
```

